{"name":"Mogging / AMOG","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":83,"casual":{"lead":["To mog someone is to outclass them in looks or in a specific trait so they appear less attractive by comparison. The word is generally said to come from AMOG, short for alpha male of the group. For looksmaxxers it frames what to improve: face, height, body frame or voice.","It is a subculture concept rather than a scientific term, and no published study tests it directly. It rests on the idea that attractiveness is judged relatively, so standing next to a more attractive or taller man changes how you are perceived. Research on social comparison and contrast effects exists more broadly."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People use the term to size up a room, a photo or a pairing, then pick goals such as gaining height, building a frame, improving the face or refining style so they come out ahead in comparisons."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Using the word carries no physical risk. Community threads and commentators link the culture around it with appearance anxiety, body dysmorphia and distress, and many posts frame ordinary encounters as contests."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"The adhesion molecule on glia (AMOG) is a homologue of the beta subunit of the Na,K-ATPase.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1688561/","source":"The Journal of cell biology","year":1990,"cites":276,"kind":null},{"title":"Biochemical and functional characterization of a novel neuron-glia adhesion molecule that is involved in neuronal migration.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2438288/","source":"The Journal of cell biology","year":1987,"cites":179,"kind":null},{"title":"Phenotypic characterization of BRCA1 and BRCA2 tumors based in a tissue microarray study with 37 immunohistochemical markers.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15770521/","source":"Breast cancer research and treatment","year":2005,"cites":124,"kind":null},{"title"